{"w":2400,"h":3390,"lines":[{"w":0.084920813242594373,"x":0.83130731062266516,"h":0.018916354559164072,"t":"Reading","y":0.044617261873027614,"c":1},{"c":1,"y":0.094395280246784008,"h":0.016069835899150564,"t":"READING PASSAGE 3","x":0.082135527007843614,"w":0.30595481872558594},{"h":0.019174041297935096,"c":1,"y":0.13067846583406906,"w":0.8234086100260416,"x":0.082135530795442835,"t":"You should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 27-40, which are based on Reading"},{"w":0.17097918192545575,"x":0.079785499193537432,"t":"Passage 3 below.","c":1,"h":0.019571267187067809,"y":0.14858560027273549},{"c":1,"h":0.024807915926682855,"x":0.17043121945789938,"t":"The persistence and peril of misinformation","y":0.19321533975116689,"w":0.65297739664713539},{"c":1,"w":0.61617477416992184,"y":0.23380075520566279,"h":0.018935157978429262,"t":"Brian Southwell looks at how human brains verify information and","x":0.18883950288176996},{"c":1,"t":"discusses some of the challenges of battling widespread falsehoods","w":0.64065709431966145,"x":0.17659137066649522,"y":0.25280236009967938,"h":0.017546477078688172},{"h":0.01606554858452458,"y":0.28924418616214165,"x":0.08008212941834103,"t":"Misinformation - both deliberately promoted and accidentally shared - is perhaps an inevitable","w":0.81108830769856766,"c":1},{"h":0.018977669909992056,"c":1,"w":0.79055440266927079,"t":"part of the world in which we live, but it is not a new problem. People likely have lied to one","x":0.080082141477265431,"y":0.30668604700599011},{"t":"another for roughly as long as verbal communication has existed. Deceiving others can offer an","y":0.32412790747119014,"w":0.81314168294270839,"h":0.018939939290724683,"c":1,"x":0.078028747752272434},{"y":0.34042527642575071,"t":"apparent opportunity to gain strategic advantage, to motivate others to action, or even to protect","h":0.021538317520006567,"x":0.077954630009354944,"c":1,"w":0.81740473429361971},{"x":0.07802874584454618,"w":0.80492813110351547,"t":"interpersonal bonds. Moreover, people inadvertently have been sharing inaccurate information","y":0.35899705028275475,"h":0.017456437845145722,"c":1},{"c":1,"h":0.015997804512316582,"t":"with one another for thousands of years.","x":0.08008213366033308,"y":0.37935103257297853,"w":0.34496920267740888},{"c":1,"x":0.08008214767088645,"w":0.78439422607421871,"y":0.4141592922679278,"t":"However, we currently live in an era in which technology enables information to reach large","h":0.016073265750851107},{"y":0.43156342202221198,"t":"audiences distributed across the globe, and thus the potential for immediate and widespread","w":0.78028747558593758,"x":0.078028746733285276,"h":0.017699115044247704,"c":1},{"w":0.74537984212239583,"y":0.44622092987205408,"h":0.020445736460277697,"x":0.078028766105126504,"c":1,"t":"effects from misinformation now looms larger than in the past. Yet the means to correct"},{"y":0.46802325592969141,"t":"misinformation might, over time, be found in those same patterns of mass communication and of","x":0.078028748272381376,"w":0.82956878662109379,"h":0.016047540737816113,"c":1},{"t":"the facilitated spread of information.","y":0.48546511626359512,"h":0.016009810118548629,"x":0.078028746720745237,"w":0.31622177124023437,"c":1},{"w":0.76796712239583331,"c":1,"t":"The main worry regarding misinformation is its potential to unduly influence attitudes and","x":0.078028759130625458,"y":0.52034883722732117,"h":0.017441860412777732},{"c":1,"w":0.77207392374674477,"t":"behavior; leading people to think and act differently than they would if they were correctly","x":0.078028746441458172,"y":0.53923303858143978,"h":0.015999519438166909},{"x":0.078028739757269816,"t":"informed, as suggested by the research teams of Stephan Lewandowsky of the University","y":0.55663716786414175,"h":0.016037250620073995,"w":0.76180699666341156,"c":1},{"t":"of Bristol and Elizabeth Marsh of Duke University, among others. In other words, we worry","h":0.017528469231979704,"c":1,"w":0.78234085083007809,"x":0.078028753214391025,"y":0.57404129817804861},{"y":0.59136216635090655,"w":0.79476221720377604,"h":0.018665989451000398,"t":"that misinformation might lead people to hold misperceptions (or false beliefs) and that these","c":1,"x":0.077979919304872095},{"x":0.078028753159891745,"w":0.83367553710937492,"h":0.019011970677558665,"c":1,"t":"misperceptions, especially when they occur among large groups of people, may have detrimental,","y":0.60901162816800136},{"w":0.6776180521647136,"t":"downstream consequences for health, social harmony, and the political climate.","h":0.017520752065652956,"c":1,"x":0.078028756267839255,"y":0.62790697676374863},{"h":0.020620097382582081,"w":0.73328180948893229,"t":"At least three observations related to misinformation in the contemporary mass-media","y":0.66043835141372254,"x":0.077940155749312984,"c":1},{"y":0.68168604662711207,"t":"environment warrant the attention of researchers, policy makers, and really everyone who","x":0.078028752607191804,"w":0.76591374715169269,"c":1,"h":0.015988371857499684},{"x":0.079973513283100595,"t":"watches television, listens to the radio, or reads information online. First of all, people who","y":0.69748429289697322,"h":0.021230921562442329,"c":1,"w":0.77433654785156247},{"y":0.71629031835569212,"x":0.077967637116494848,"h":0.019370371050539292,"t":"encounter misinformation tend to believe it, at least initially. Secondly, electronic and print","c":1,"w":0.77631037394205737},{"w":0.81724848429361963,"h":0.017508746459420799,"c":1,"y":0.7353982302559452,"x":0.078028735174798095,"t":"media often do not block many types of misinformation before it appears in content available to"},{"w":0.8173894246419271,"c":1,"y":0.75142253755700994,"h":0.019572203419553125,"x":0.077955729089020834,"t":"large audiences. Thirdly, countering misinformation once it has enjoyed wide exposure can be a"},{"w":0.21800168355305993,"x":0.077886463671077003,"y":0.77049315945727803,"h":0.01591599838571911,"c":1,"t":"resource-intensive effort."},{"h":0.016027817402617384,"c":1,"x":0.080082147113991295,"t":"Knowing what happens when people initially encounter misinformation holds tremendous","w":0.76796712239583331,"y":0.80668604662715049},{"c":1,"w":0.82135523478190098,"x":0.078028748069704265,"y":0.82412790709120254,"h":0.015990086783350121,"t":"importance for estimating the potential for subsequent problems. Although it is fairly routine for"},{"y":0.84156976746444101,"x":0.078028754720706764,"h":0.017441860412777732,"t":"individuals to come across information that is false, the question of exactly how - and when - we","w":0.8316221618652343,"c":1},{"c":1,"t":"mentally label information as true or false has garnered philosophical debate. The dilemma is","y":0.86046511639457968,"w":0.79671457926432288,"h":0.015988371857499684,"x":0.078028747232806575},{"w":0.82751541137695306,"h":0.016019242773365416,"c":1,"t":"neatly summarized by a contrast between how the 17th-century philosophers René Descartes and","y":0.87787610594373777,"x":0.078028741865522777},{"y":0.89311769882687497,"c":1,"t":"Baruch Spinoza described human information engagement, with conflicting predictions that only","h":0.020171751174251629,"w":0.82355962117513004,"x":0.080007334598364815},{"w":0.028747431437174509,"t":"25","y":0.92296511634307521,"h":0.014534883302221635,"c":1,"x":0.88501026789537585},{"y":0.94166666659226195,"h":0.04166666666666663,"t":"IELTSXpress.com","x":0.51176469619230891,"w":0.45882354736328124,"c":1}]}